ABSTRACT
This project explores how blockchain technology can support digital asset integrity verification and tokenisation. The application calculates a SHA-256 fingerprint of the African Leadership University (ALU) logo image and records that fingerprint in an Ethereum-compatible smart contract.
The ALUAssetRegistry smart contract uses the ERC-721 standard to mint a non-fungible token (NFT) for a registered digital asset and stores associated metadata, including its name, file type, content hash, registering address, and registration timestamp. A separate ALULogoToken smart contract uses the ERC-20 standard to issue ALUT tokens representing shares in the project's tokenisation model.
The contracts were compiled and tested using Hardhat. All eight assignment-specific tests passed, while the complete local test run reported thirteen passing tests, including the default Hardhat sample tests. The deployment script also executed successfully on a local simulated blockchain.
The project demonstrates how cryptographic hashing, smart contracts, NFTs, and fungible tokens can work together to create a basic digital asset registry and tokenisation system. However, the implementation is an educational prototype, and the local deployment does not constitute a deployment to a public Ethereum testnet.

1. INTRODUCTION
   Digital images can be copied, distributed, or modified without obvious visible differences. This creates challenges when attempting to verify whether a file matches a previously registered version. Blockchain technology and cryptographic hashing can help address this problem by providing a way to record and compare the digital fingerprint of an asset.
   A cryptographic hash is a fixed-length value generated from digital data. When a file changes, even slightly, its hash will normally change. By calculating the SHA-256 hash of an image and recording it in a smart contract, a system can later compare the hash of a submitted file with the registered value.
   The ALU Logo Blockchain project applies this concept to the ALU logo. It also demonstrates tokenisation through two Ethereum token standards: ERC-721 for representing a unique registered asset and ERC-20 for representing fungible token units.
   1.1 Project Objectives
   The main objectives of this project are:
   Calculate the SHA-256 hash of the ALU logo image.
   Register the logo and its metadata through an ERC-721-based smart contract.
   Prevent duplicate registration of the same content hash.
   Provide a verification function to compare a supplied hash with the registered value.
   Implement an ERC-20 token with an initial supply of one million tokens.
   Demonstrate owner-controlled token distribution and ownership percentage calculations.
   Compile, test, and deploy the contracts locally using Hardhat.
