3. DIGITAL FINGERPRINT AND ASSET REGISTRATION
   3.1 SHA-256 Hash Generation
   The file alu-logo.png was processed using the SHA-256 hashing algorithm. The resulting hash acts as a digital fingerprint of the file's contents.
   The SHA-256 hash calculated for the project logo is:
   407BE7B9AC994BCCA2AB5E0C901B6471A149AA08FC9201F7BF546E6852FB9DDE
   When represented as a hexadecimal value for interaction with the smart contract, it is stored as:
   0x407be7b9ac994bcca2ab5e0c901b6471a149aa08fc9201f7bf546e6852fb9dde
   The hash was generated locally from the image file rather than by uploading the image itself to the smart contract.
   The following PowerShell command can be used to reproduce the calculation:
   Get-FileHash .\alu-logo.png -Algorithm SHA256
   3.2 Why Hashing Matters
   Hashing provides a way to detect changes to a file. If a different version of the image is submitted, its calculated hash can be compared with the original registered hash.
   If the hashes match, this supports the conclusion that the file contents are identical, assuming the same hashing algorithm and correct handling of the file. If they differ, the file contents differ.
   The smart contract records the hash supplied to it. It does not independently calculate the SHA-256 hash of an uploaded image. Therefore, verification requires calculating the hash of the file being checked and comparing it with the registered value.
4. SMART CONTRACT EXPLANATION
   The project implements two principal smart contracts: ALUAssetRegistry.sol and ALULogoToken.sol.
   4.1 ALUAssetRegistry.sol — ERC-721 Asset Registry
   The ALUAssetRegistry contract uses the ERC-721 standard to represent registered assets as non-fungible tokens.
   Each successfully registered asset receives a unique token ID. The contract associates that token ID with metadata describing the asset.
   The metadata includes:
   Asset name
   File type
   Content hash
   Address that registered the asset
   Registration timestamp
   Main Functionality
   Asset registration: The registration function accepts the asset name, file type, and content hash. It creates a new NFT and associates the metadata with that token.
   Duplicate prevention: The contract checks whether a content hash has already been registered. If it has, another registration using the same hash is rejected.
   Hash verification: The verification function compares a supplied hash with the registered hash and returns a verification result.
   Metadata retrieval: The getAsset function allows the stored metadata associated with a token ID to be retrieved.
   NFT ownership: The ERC-721 standard provides functionality for identifying the address that owns a particular token.
   During the local deployment demonstration, the logo was registered using the name “ALU Official Logo” and the file type “PNG.” The resulting NFT was assigned token ID 1.
   4.2 ALULogoToken.sol — ERC-20 Token
   The ALULogoToken contract implements the ERC-20 standard using OpenZeppelin's implementation and ownership functionality.
   The token has the following properties:
   Token name: ALU Logo Token
   Token symbol: ALUT
   Initial supply: 1,000,000 ALUT tokens
   Token standard: ERC-20
   Main Functionality
   Initial token supply: The contract mints one million tokens to the designated owner during deployment. The initial supply accounts for the token's decimal precision.
   Share distribution: The owner-only distributeShares function allows the owner to distribute tokens to designated recipient addresses.
   Ownership percentage: The ownershipPercentage function calculates a whole-number percentage based on an address's token balance relative to the total token supply.
   For example, an address holding 100,000 tokens out of a total supply of 1,000,000 would hold 10% of the token supply under this model.
   The ERC-20 tokens demonstrate a technical model for distributing fungible units. Their existence does not automatically grant legally enforceable equity, rights to the university's logo, or official ownership of the university.
5. TOKENISATION MODEL
   Tokenisation involves representing an asset or a unit of value through a digital token. This project demonstrates two different tokenisation approaches.
   5.1 Non-Fungible Token (ERC-721)
   The ERC-721 token represents a unique registered digital asset record. In this project, the NFT identifies the ALU logo registration and links it to the stored metadata.
   The NFT is useful for identifying a particular registered asset because each token has a unique token ID.
   5.2 Fungible Token (ERC-20)
   The ERC-20 token represents interchangeable units. Each ALUT token follows the same token rules and can be transferred between compatible addresses.
   The token contract demonstrates an initial supply of one million tokens, owner-controlled distribution, and balance-based ownership percentage calculations.
   5.3 Difference Between the Two Standards
   ERC-721 is used to represent a unique asset record, while ERC-20 is used to represent fungible token units.
   The two contracts therefore serve complementary purposes. The NFT identifies the registered logo record, while the fungible token demonstrates how token units can be distributed among different addresses.
   The project does not establish legal ownership of the original logo or provide a legal framework for equity. Those matters would require separate authorisation and governance arrangements.
