9. CHALLENGES AND SOLUTIONS
   9.1 Hardhat 3 Configuration
   One challenge was working with the Hardhat 3 development environment, whose plugin and network APIs differ from those found in many Hardhat 2 tutorials.
   The project was configured to use the Hardhat Toolbox Mocha/Ethers plugin. The test and deployment files use the Hardhat 3 network connection API to interact with the simulated blockchain.
   9.2 Hash Representation
   The SHA-256 digest consists of 32 bytes, typically displayed as 64 hexadecimal characters. Solidity's bytes32 type provides a suitable representation for storing the digest.
   The project uses a 0x-prefixed hexadecimal string when supplying the hash to the contract. The same representation is used when retrieving and comparing the stored value.
   9.3 Understanding Token Standards
   ERC-721 and ERC-20 serve different purposes. ERC-721 identifies unique tokenised records, whereas ERC-20 represents interchangeable token units.
   Implementing both standards helped demonstrate the difference between registering a unique digital asset and distributing fungible tokens.
   9.4 Testing and Deployment Scope
   The contracts were tested and deployed locally. This made it possible to validate the contract logic without requiring a public blockchain deployment.
   However, local testing does not establish production security or prove that the contracts have been deployed to a public Ethereum testnet. Those would require additional work.
10. LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE IMPROVEMENTS
    The project is an educational prototype. Several improvements could make it more suitable for real-world use.
    First, the contracts could be deployed to a public Ethereum testnet, and their source code could be verified using a public block explorer.
    Second, a web interface could be developed to allow users to register assets, calculate file hashes, and verify images without directly interacting with the command line.
    Third, additional tests could cover more edge cases, access-control scenarios, invalid inputs, and unexpected contract interactions.
    Fourth, a real deployment would require a clear authorisation process for registering assets, particularly where an organisation's logo is involved.
    Finally, the tokenisation model would require clearly defined legal and governance terms before being used to represent actual ownership or financial rights.
11. CONCLUSION
    The ALU Logo Blockchain project demonstrates how cryptographic hashing and blockchain smart contracts can be combined to register a digital asset and support integrity verification.
    The ALUAssetRegistry contract uses ERC-721 to create a unique token associated with the logo's metadata and content hash. The ALULogoToken contract uses ERC-20 to demonstrate fungible token issuance, distribution, and ownership percentage calculations.
    The contracts compiled successfully, all eight assignment-specific tests passed, and the deployment script completed successfully on a local simulated blockchain.
    The project illustrates the potential of blockchain technology for digital asset registration and tokenisation. It also highlights important limitations: recording a hash does not independently prove that an asset is authentic or officially authorised, and a local deployment does not demonstrate operation on a public blockchain.
    Overall, the project provided practical experience with Solidity, Ethereum token standards, cryptographic hashing, automated testing, local deployment, and Git-based source code management.
